{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reinstates penalties for possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth. Under HB4002, individuals caught with these drugs can face either criminal charges or an alternative path involving a behavioral health intervention and a diversion process to avoid fines. Possession remains a misdemeanor, carrying a potential jail term of up to half a year, and stricter rules now apply to sales near areas like playgrounds and schools.\n\n***\n\nThe updated law promotes, but doesn’t require, local jurisdictions to establish health-focused alternatives to incarceration. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacities, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. Police are instructed to offer a chance at intervention only if the person has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable. If those conditions are met, officers can contact a dedicated line to summon a mobile health team.\n\n***\n\nTime is critical: the health team must arrive within 30 minutes. If they don’t, the individual will be taken into custody. Officers aren’t permitted to delay enforcement indefinitely, meaning the window for avoiding prosecution is narrow and logistically demanding. This creates pressure on local services to respond quickly and efficiently, even in areas with limited resources.\n\n***\n\nIn a country where access to care varies widely, the success of this model hinges on coordination—not just between law enforcement and health workers, but also on how quickly support can reach someone in crisis. Whether someone can get help before being arrested may depend on where they live, how old they are, or even how far the nearest team must travel. It’s not about shopping for solutions—it’s about knowing when to act. The tongue of policy speaks loudly, but the age of compassion must guide its tone.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform matters, the country watches closely, we must learn to eat our pride, stop shopping for quick fixes, and listen with our tongue to those most affected.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9479886293411255}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.058058079332113266}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.040276382118463516}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.06856127083301544}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.9920417070388794}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.5003767609596252}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -1.0918711423873901}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.006387058645486832}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.07930146157741547}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.04622189328074455}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.0001591317413840443}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0035622967407107353}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.00150245754